# Deploying Cybernetics Projects to K3s-server

This runbook covers building the `cybernetics-project-*` images on this dev machine,
shipping them to the k3s node, and rolling them out to the `plane` namespace. Each
phase is one bash block. Paste the blocks in order into **one terminal**: later blocks
use the variables set by earlier ones. The release plan is also saved to
`~/.cyb-release`, so a new terminal can resume with `source ~/.cyb-release`.

Only one step needs you at the keyboard: phase 5, the containerd import. It runs
`sudo` on the server and asks for your password. Everything else runs unattended.

The server-side runbook (cluster layout, domain/TLS, database resets) lives on the
server at `~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment/apps/plane/readme.md`. This file is the dev-machine
counterpart, and it follows the same release procedure.

---

## 1. How the deployment works

```
dev machine                                   K3s-server (k8s-micro-server, 192.168.0.103)
───────────                                   ──────────────────────────────────────────────
docker build ─▶ cybernetics-project-*:<tag>
docker save | zstd ──── ssh ────────────────▶ ~/cyb-<tag>.tar.zst
                                              sudo k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import  (you)
                                              sed new tags into ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment/apps/plane/*.yaml
                                              migrator Job (backend only)
                                              kubectl apply -k apps/plane/
                                              host Caddy :443 ─▶ NodePort 30808 ─▶ plane/proxy ─▶ pods
```

- **No registry.** Every `cybernetics-project-*` Deployment uses `imagePullPolicy: Never`.
  An image has to be imported into the node's containerd (namespace `k8s.io`) before a
  rollout, otherwise the pod fails with `ErrImageNeverPull`.
- **Deployments use `strategy: Recreate`.** A rollout stops the old pod before starting
  the new one, so expect 30–90 seconds of downtime per rolled-out deployment.
- **The manifests are the source of truth.** Rollouts edit the image tags in the
  kustomize repo and run `kubectl apply -k`. Never use `kubectl set image`: the
  next `apply -k` would silently revert it.
- **Tag format:** `release.<UTC timestamp>.1`, for example `release.2026-10-06T11-56-45Z.1`.
  All components built in one release share the same tag.

### Component matrix

| Component | Image | Dockerfile | Build context | Server manifest(s) | Deployments | Needs migrator |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `backend` | `cybernetics-project-backend` | `apps/api/Dockerfile.api` | `apps/api` | `backend.yaml`, `migrator-job.yaml` | `api`, `worker`, `beat-worker` | **yes** |
| `web` | `cybernetics-project-web` | `apps/web/Dockerfile.web` | `.` | `frontend.yaml` | `web` | no |
| `space` | `cybernetics-project-space` | `apps/space/Dockerfile.space` | `.` | `frontend.yaml` | `space` | no |
| `admin` | `cybernetics-project-admin` | `apps/admin/Dockerfile.admin` | `.` | `frontend.yaml` | `admin` | no |
| `live` | `cybernetics-project-live` | `apps/live/Dockerfile.live` | `.` | `realtime.yaml` | `live` | no |
| `proxy` | `cybernetics-project-proxy` | `apps/proxy/Dockerfile.ce` | `apps/proxy` | `proxy.yaml` | `proxy` | no |

`iframely` uses the upstream `makeplane/iframely` image and is not built here.

### Which components to ship

| You changed… | Ship |
|---|---|
| `apps/api/**` (Django, Celery, MCP server) | `backend` |
| `apps/web/**`, or `packages/**` used by web (constants, i18n, propel, ui, …) | `web` |
| `apps/space/**`, `apps/admin/**`, `apps/live/**` | the matching component |
| `packages/**` used by several apps | every app that imports the changed package |
| `apps/proxy/Caddyfile.ce` | `proxy` |
| A new backend endpoint and the UI that calls it | `backend` **and** `web`, in the same release |

Not sure? Compare against the last release's commit, which phase 2 prints. Look at
`git diff --stat <last-release-commit>..HEAD -- apps packages`.

