## 2. Prerequisites (one-off)

- Docker, `zstd`, `curl` and `python3` on this machine.
- An SSH alias `K3s-server` in `~/.ssh/config` with key-based login (`BatchMode` must work).
- Your user on the server can run `kubectl` without sudo (kubeconfig in `~/.kube`).
- Your server password, for the `sudo` import in phase 5.
- Optional: a Plane API token in `PLANE_TOKEN`, for the MCP check in phase 7.

---

## 3. Phase-by-phase

### Phase 0: preflight checks

Checks tools, SSH, kubectl, disk space and that the working tree is clean. Stops at the first
problem.

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
for cmd in docker zstd curl python3 ssh git; do
  command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null || { echo "missing: $cmd"; false; }
done
docker info >/dev/null && echo "docker: ok"
ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get deploy >/dev/null && echo "ssh + kubectl: ok"; df -h ~ | tail -1'
git status --short -- apps packages | grep -v '^??' && echo "WARNING: uncommitted changes in apps/ or packages/ will be baked into the images" || echo "git: tracked files clean"
echo "HEAD: $(git log -1 --format='%h %s')"
```

### Phase 1: plan the release

Set **which components** to ship (space-separated, see the matrix). This block fixes the
tag, defines the component mapping, and saves the plan to `~/.cyb-release`.

```bash
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
COMPONENTS="backend web"                       # <- edit: any of backend web space admin live proxy
T="release.$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H-%M-%SZ).1"
COMMIT="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"

cat > ~/.cyb-release <<EOF
export T="$T" COMPONENTS="$COMPONENTS" COMMIT="$COMMIT" REPO="$PWD"
dockerfile_of() { case "\$1" in backend) echo apps/api/Dockerfile.api;; web) echo apps/web/Dockerfile.web;;
  space) echo apps/space/Dockerfile.space;; admin) echo apps/admin/Dockerfile.admin;;
  live) echo apps/live/Dockerfile.live;; proxy) echo apps/proxy/Dockerfile.ce;; *) return 1;; esac; }
context_of() { case "\$1" in backend) echo apps/api;; proxy) echo apps/proxy;; web|space|admin|live) echo .;; *) return 1;; esac; }
images_of() { local c; for c in \$COMPONENTS; do printf 'cybernetics-project-%s:%s ' "\$c" "\$T"; done; }
EOF
source ~/.cyb-release
for c in $COMPONENTS; do dockerfile_of "$c" >/dev/null || { echo "unknown component: $c"; false; }; done
echo "release $T  commit $COMMIT  components: $COMPONENTS"
echo "currently deployed:"
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get deploy -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image --no-headers'
```

### Phase 2: build the images

Builds every planned component. A failed build stops the loop. Logs are written to
`/tmp/cyb-build-<component>.log`.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release && cd "$REPO"
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  echo "== building $c -> cybernetics-project-$c:$T"
  if ! docker build -f "$(dockerfile_of "$c")" -t "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" \
      --label "org.opencontainers.image.revision=$COMMIT" "$(context_of "$c")" \
      > "/tmp/cyb-build-$c.log" 2>&1; then
    echo "   BUILD FAILED: $c (full log: /tmp/cyb-build-$c.log)"; tail -20 "/tmp/cyb-build-$c.log"
    break
  fi
  echo "   ok"
done
docker images --format '{{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}  {{.Size}}' | grep -F "$T"
```

### Phase 3: smoke-test the images locally

These checks catch a broken image before it reaches the server. The backend test boots
Django with the test settings and builds the MCP server. It needs no database.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  case "$c" in
    backend)
      docker run --rm --entrypoint sh -e DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=plane.settings.test \
        -e SECRET_KEY=smoke -e REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379 "cybernetics-project-backend:$T" -c '
        python manage.py check >/dev/null && echo "   backend: manage.py check ok"
        python -c "
import asyncio, django; django.setup()
from django.conf import settings; settings.MCP_READ_ONLY=False; settings.MCP_TOOLSETS=[]
from plane.mcp.server import build_server
print(\"   backend: django ok, MCP tools:\", len(asyncio.run(build_server().list_tools())))"' ;;
    web|space|admin)
      docker run --rm --entrypoint sh "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" -c \
        'test -s /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html && echo "   '"$c"': index.html ok, $(find /usr/share/nginx/html -name "*.js" | wc -l) js files"' ;;
    *)
      docker image inspect "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" >/dev/null && echo "   $c: image present" ;;
  esac
done
```

To check that a particular change made it into the web bundle, grep for a new string:
`docker run --rm --entrypoint sh cybernetics-project-web:$T -c 'grep -rl "<new string>" /usr/share/nginx/html | head -1'`

### Phase 4: ship to the server

Streams the images, zstd-compressed, straight into a tarball on the server, then
checks the archive. The backend and web images together compress to about 160 MB.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
set -o pipefail
docker save $(images_of) | zstd -T0 -3 -q | ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "cat > ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst" \
  && ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "zstd -t -q ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && ls -lh ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && echo integrity-ok"
set +o pipefail
```

### Phase 5: import into containerd (you, needs your password)

This step needs `sudo`, so you run it yourself. Print the exact command:

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
echo "ssh -t K3s-server 'zstd -dc ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst | sudo k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import -'"
```

Run the printed command in a terminal. From inside Claude Code, prefix it with `! `. It
prints one `unpacking docker.io/library/cybernetics-project-…` line per image.

Then confirm the node has the images. This uses kubectl, so no sudo:

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "kubectl get node k8s-micro-server -o jsonpath='{range .status.images[*]}{.names}{\"\n\"}{end}'" \
  | grep -o "cybernetics-project-[a-z]*:$T" | sort -u
# Expect one line per component in $COMPONENTS
```

### Phase 6: roll out

This block runs entirely on the server. In order, it:

1. **Records the release.** Copies the current manifests to `~/plane-releases/<tag>/` (the
   rollback point) and writes a `release.txt` with the old and new images.
2. **Bumps the tags.** Rewrites the image tag of each component in its manifest(s), and
   fails if any manifest wasn't updated. The sed expression matches `cybernetics-project-<c>:<anything>`,
   so a hand-edited ad-hoc name (like the old `plane-web:6-oct-2026`) has to be renamed
   first.
3. **Checks the node.** Every new image must be on the node, otherwise it aborts and
   restores the manifests.
4. **Shows the diff.** Runs `kubectl diff -k` and prints only the changed `image:` lines, plus a
   count of any *other* changed lines. Other changes mean the live cluster and the repo
   have drifted; read them before continuing.
5. **Migrates.** If `backend` is in the release, deletes and re-creates the `migrator` Job and
   waits for it to complete (up to 15 minutes). A failed migration stops the rollout before
   any app pod is replaced.
6. **Rolls out.** Runs `kubectl apply -k apps/plane/` and waits for each affected deployment.

Set `DRY_RUN=1` to stop after step 4. A dry run restores the manifests and leaves the
cluster unchanged. Always do a dry run first.

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
DRY_RUN=1                                   # <- set to 0 for the real rollout
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "DRY_RUN=$DRY_RUN bash -s -- '$T' $COMPONENTS" <<'REMOTE'
set -euo pipefail
T="$1"; shift; COMPONENTS="$*"
cd ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment
APP=apps/plane
REL=~/plane-releases/$T

manifests_of() { case "$1" in backend) echo "backend.yaml migrator-job.yaml";; web|space|admin) echo frontend.yaml;;
                 live) echo realtime.yaml;; proxy) echo proxy.yaml;; *) return 1;; esac; }
deploys_of()   { case "$1" in backend) echo "api worker beat-worker";; *) echo "$1";; esac; }
restore()      { cp "$REL"/*.yaml "$APP"/; echo "   manifests restored from $REL"; }

echo "== 1. recording release in $REL"
mkdir -p "$REL"
cp "$APP"/*.yaml "$REL"/
{ echo "release: $T"; echo "date: $(date -u +%FT%TZ)"; echo "components: $COMPONENTS"
  echo "previous images:"; grep -h "image: cybernetics-project-" "$APP"/*.yaml | sort -u | sed 's/^ */  /'; } > "$REL/release.txt"

echo "== 2. bumping image tags"
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  for f in $(manifests_of "$c"); do
    sed -i -E "s#(image: cybernetics-project-$c:)[^[:space:]\"']+#\1$T#" "$APP/$f"
    grep -q "image: cybernetics-project-$c:$T" "$APP/$f" || { echo "   tag not set for $c in $f"; restore; exit 1; }
    echo "   $c -> $f"
  done
done

echo "== 3. checking images on the node"
NODE_IMAGES="$(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*].status.images[*]}{.names}{"\n"}{end}')"
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  grep -q "cybernetics-project-$c:$T" <<<"$NODE_IMAGES" || { echo "   MISSING on node: cybernetics-project-$c:$T (run the phase 5 import)"; restore; exit 1; }
  echo "   present: cybernetics-project-$c:$T"
done

echo "== 4. kubectl diff"
DIFF="$(kubectl diff -k "$APP"/ 2>&1 || true)"
grep -E '^[-+] +image:' <<<"$DIFF" || echo "   (no image changes?)"
OTHER="$( { grep -E '^[-+][^-+]' <<<"$DIFF" || true; } \
  | { grep -v -E 'image:|generation:|resourceVersion|managedFields|time:|^[-+] +f:' || true; } | wc -l)"
if [ "$OTHER" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "   NOTE: $OTHER other changed line(s): the cluster has drifted from the repo; review with: kubectl diff -k $APP/"
fi

if [ "${DRY_RUN:-1}" = "1" ]; then
  restore; rm -rf "$REL"; echo "== DRY RUN complete, nothing applied"; exit 0
fi

if grep -qw backend <<<"$COMPONENTS"; then
  echo "== 5. running migrations"
  kubectl delete job migrator -n plane --ignore-not-found
  kubectl apply -f "$APP/migrator-job.yaml"
  if ! kubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/migrator -n plane --timeout=900s; then
    kubectl -n plane logs job/migrator --tail=40 || true
    echo "   MIGRATION FAILED: app pods were NOT replaced. Manifests still carry the new tag;"
    echo "   restore them with: cp $REL/*.yaml ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment/$APP/"
    exit 1
  fi
  kubectl -n plane logs job/migrator --tail=5
fi

echo "== 6. applying and rolling out"
kubectl apply -k "$APP"/ | grep -v unchanged || true
for c in $COMPONENTS; do
  for d in $(deploys_of "$c"); do kubectl -n plane rollout status "deploy/$d" --timeout=300s; done
done
kubectl -n plane get deploy -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,READY:.status.readyReplicas,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image
echo "== rollout complete; rollback point: $REL"
REMOTE
```

### Phase 7: verify

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
URL=https://projects.avarile.com
echo "web:       $(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' $URL/)"                  # 200
echo "instances: $(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' $URL/api/instances/)"    # 200
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane get pods --no-headers | grep -v -E "Running|Completed" || echo "pods: all Running"'
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server 'kubectl -n plane logs deploy/api --since=5m | grep -i -E "traceback|exception|\"levelname\": \"ERROR\"" | head -10 || true'

# MCP endpoint (only when PLANE_TOKEN is set), expects the server's tool count
if [ -n "${PLANE_TOKEN:-}" ]; then
  curl -s -X POST "$URL/api/mcp" -H "Authorization: Bearer $PLANE_TOKEN" \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \
    -H 'MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18' -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}' \
    | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print("mcp tools:", len(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["tools"]))'
fi
```

If anything looks wrong, go to [Rollback](#4-rollback). The rollback takes about a minute.

### Phase 8: clean up

```bash
source ~/.cyb-release
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "rm -f ~/cyb-$T.tar.zst && echo 'server tarball removed'"
# Record the release in the server's kustomize repo (commit the bumped tags):
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "cd ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment && git add apps/plane && git commit -q -m 'plane: release $T ($COMMIT)' && git log -1 --oneline"
```

Old images are deliberately kept, so the previous release can be rolled back. To free
space now and then, remove images older than the last two releases.

On this machine:

```bash
docker images --format '{{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}' | grep '^cybernetics-project-' | sort   # pick old tags, then:
# docker rmi cybernetics-project-backend:release.<old>.1 ...
```

On the server, this needs sudo, so run it yourself:

```bash
ssh -t K3s-server 'sudo k3s crictl images | grep cybernetics-project'
# ssh -t K3s-server 'sudo k3s crictl rmi docker.io/library/cybernetics-project-backend:release.<old>.1'
```

Never remove an image that a Deployment still references, or that you may roll back to.

