## 4. Rollback

Every rollout saves the manifests it replaced in `~/plane-releases/<tag>/`. Rolling
back puts them back and re-applies them. The previous images are still in containerd,
so there's nothing to rebuild or import.

```bash
ROLLBACK_TAG="release.YYYY-MM-DDTHH-MM-SSZ.1"   # <- the release to undo (ls ~/plane-releases on the server)
ssh -o BatchMode=yes K3s-server "bash -s -- '$ROLLBACK_TAG'" <<'REMOTE'
set -euo pipefail
REL=~/plane-releases/$1
[ -d "$REL" ] || { echo "no rollback record at $REL"; ls ~/plane-releases; exit 1; }
cat "$REL/release.txt"
cd ~/dev-ops/k3s-deployment
cp "$REL"/*.yaml apps/plane/
kubectl apply -k apps/plane/ | grep -v unchanged || true
for d in api worker beat-worker web space admin live proxy; do kubectl -n plane rollout status deploy/$d --timeout=300s; done
kubectl -n plane get deploy -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image
REMOTE
```

**Migrations are not reverted.** If the release ran a schema migration, the old backend
must still work with the new schema. Additive migrations are fine. For a destructive
migration, reverse it before rolling back:
`kubectl -n plane exec deploy/api -- python manage.py migrate db <previous_migration>`.

